# Math Engine

Pure TypeScript. No side effects, no I/O, no dates read from the system clock, no randomness. Every function takes inputs and returns data.

## Purity

- No imports from React, Netlify, storage, `fetch`, `fs`, or any database client.
- No reads of `Date.now()`, `Math.random()`, `process.env`, or `localStorage`. If a calculation needs the current date, it is passed in as a parameter.
- Functions return data. They never write anywhere.

## Determinism

The same inputs must always produce the same outputs. This is the product's central claim and it is testable — snapshot tests in `tests/` freeze known inputs to known outputs.

Never add a model, heuristic, scoring function, or AI call. Winning-path status is rule precedence over named alerts, not a composite score. See `alerts.ts`.

## Calibration boundary

`src/calibration.ts` defines the SHAPE of a calibration profile. It MUST NOT contain values.

- `NULL_CALIBRATION_PROFILE` stays empty. Do not populate it with "sensible defaults."
- Empirical estimates and alert thresholds belong in the private `@wings/calibration-profiles` package, passed in at runtime.
- The engine never fetches a profile. It receives one.

## Registries

`metricRegistry.ts` and `assumptionRegistry.ts` hold stable keys shared by UI, storage, tests, and audit. Adding a metric or assumption means adding a registry entry. Never introduce a bare string key at a call site.

Formula IDs carry a version suffix (`victory.vote_goal.v0.2`). Changing a formula's behavior means a new formula ID and a bump to `MATH_ENGINE_VERSION` in `index.ts`. Changing an empirical default is a calibration profile change, not an engine change.

## Evidence

Every assumption carries an `EvidenceClass`. `PRODUCT_PLACEHOLDER` means provisional and the UI must label it. `isCustomerFacingPermitted()` in `calibration.ts` gates restricted sources — never bypass it.

## Universe and capacity

Strategic Desired Universe, Reachable Universe, and Capacity-Supported Universe are distinct stages. Never collapse them. Reachability gap and capacity gap are reported separately because they have different remedies.

Capacity uses shared campaign resource pools with explicit channel allocations. Two channels sharing a pool MUST NOT each see the full pool. Over-allocation is an `ALLOCATION` constraint, distinct from a `CAPACITY` shortfall. See `docs/PROGRAM_BUDGET_CAPACITY_MODEL.md` before touching `programBudget.ts` or `capacity.ts`.

Where a flake rate is modeled, distinguish completed shifts from scheduled shifts. Calendar-facing numbers use scheduled.

## Tests

Tests are `.cjs` files under `tests/`. Every new formula gets a unit test. Every fixture uses invented round numbers, never plausible real-world ones.
